.Dd November 9, 2026
.Dt VMMANAGER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vmmanager
.Nd terminal interface for vmd virtual machines
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl \-version | \-man | \-install\-man
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a full-screen terminal program for creating and running virtual machines
with
.Xr vmd 8 .
It lists every VM that
.Xr vmd 8
knows about, with its live state from
.Xr vmctl 8 ,
and offers actions on the VMs that belong to the user running it.
.Pp
Each VM gets one folder,
.Pa ~/vmmanager/<name> ,
holding its disk and a small
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5
file.
The VM's configuration stays in
.Xr vm.conf 5 ,
which remains the source of truth for everything
.Xr vmd 8
understands.
.Nm
only runs on
.Ox .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl \-man
Show this manual page with
.Ic man \-l
without installing it.
This works on any system.
.It Fl \-install\-man
Install this page and
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5
into
.Pa /usr/local/man
and rebuild the database with
.Xr makewhatis 8 .
Needs
.Xr doas 1 .
.El
.Pp
On first start, users who may use
.Xr doas 1
are asked once whether to install the man pages system-wide.
Answering no is remembered and the question is not asked again.
After an upgrade, users who did not decline are asked again.
.Sh KEYS
The main list accepts these keys.
Actions apply to the selected VM and work only when they make sense for its
state.
The footer lists all keys; pressing one that does not apply to the selected VM
explains why.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic n
Create a VM.
Asks for name, memory (default 1G), CPUs (1), disk size (20G), an optional
install ISO and whether the VM gets Internet access (default on).
The name must start with a letter and contain only letters, digits,
.Sq \&. ,
.Sq _
and
.Sq \- .
.It Ic s
Start the VM in the background.
.It Ic r
Run: start the VM and attach its serial console.
.It Ic o
Connect: attach the console of a running VM.
.It Ic x
Stop the VM.
The first stop is graceful; if the VM stays up,
.Nm
offers to force it.
.It Ic e
Update memory, CPUs, ISO and the Internet setting of a stopped VM.
Any other line of the VM's block is kept as written and shown read-only.
.It Ic d
Delete a stopped VM.
The configuration is removed first.
The VM's folder, with its disk, is kept unless you agree to remove it.
Creating a VM whose name still has a folder is refused.
.It Ic f
Share a host folder with the VM, or stop sharing it (see
.Sx SHARED FOLDERS ) .
Needs Internet on for the VM.
.It Ic m
Migrate a VM that was not created by
.Nm
(see
.Sx MIGRATING ) .
.It Ic \&?
Show this manual page.
.It Ic q
Quit.
.El
.Pp
On a VM console, type
.Ql ~.
at the start of a line to leave it and return to
.Nm .
.Ql ~^D
is also accepted, as printed by
.Xr vmctl 8
console.
.Pp
VMs owned by other users are listed but have no actions.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>/<name>.qcow2
The VM's disk, created with
.Ic vmctl create .
.It Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>/vmmanager.toml
Extras that
.Xr vm.conf 5
cannot hold.
See
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5 .
.It Pa ~/vmmanager/state.toml
Tool state, such as the installed man page version.
.It Pa /etc/vm.conf
Gets one added line,
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf" ,
under a
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment.
Migrated blocks are commented out with the marker
.Li # moved to vmmanager YYYY-MM-DD:\ .
Nothing else is changed.
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf
Regenerated: one include line per managed VM.
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf
The
.Ic vm
block of one managed VM.
Blocks created by
.Nm
always have
.Ic owner <user>
and
.Ic disable ,
so
.Xr vmd 8
never starts them by itself.
Migrated blocks get
.Ic owner
added if missing and otherwise keep their original lines, including their own
.Ic disable
or lack of it.
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf
The NAT rules, loaded as a
.Xr pf.conf 5
anchor.
.It Pa /etc/pf.conf
Gets a marked 3-line block, see
.Sx NETWORKING .
.It Pa /etc/sysctl.conf
Gets the line
.Li net.inet.ip.forwarding=1
whenever it is missing from the file, whatever the running value is.
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.share
The folder shared with one VM, one absolute path.
See
.Sx SHARED FOLDERS .
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/sync-exports
The script that writes the lines of
.Nm
in
.Pa /etc/exports ,
run by
.Nm
and from
.Pa /etc/rc.local .
.It Pa /etc/exports
Gets two lines per shared VM, see
.Sx SHARED FOLDERS .
.It Pa /etc/rc.local
Gets one added line that runs the sync script at boot, under a
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment.
.It Pa /var/run/exports.lock
Keeps two runs of the sync script apart.
.It Pa /usr/local/man/man8/vmmanager.8 , /usr/local/man/man5/vmmanager.toml.5
The installed pages.
.El
.Pp
Files under
.Pa /etc/vmmanager
are
.Li root:wheel
mode 0644 and directories 0755, except
.Pa sync-exports ,
which is 0755.
Existing files keep their mode.
.Sh PRIVILEGES
Listing VMs needs access to the
.Xr vmd 8
socket, which wheel members have.
For other users, the administrator must set
.Ic socket owner
in
.Xr vm.conf 5 .
.Pp
Starting, stopping and attaching consoles on your own VMs needs no root.
Creating, updating, deleting and migrating VMs, setting up the network,
enabling
.Xr vmd 8
and installing the man pages use
.Xr doas 1 .
Each root step is announced before the prompt.
.Pp
Without the
.Ic persist
option in
.Xr doas.conf 5 ,
every root step asks for the password.
Recommended:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
permit persist :wheel
.Ed
.Pp
Every change to
.Pa /etc
is checked with
.Ic vmd \-n
(or
.Ic pfctl \-nf
for pf).
If the check fails, every changed file is restored exactly as it was and the
error text is shown.
If
.Xr vmd 8
cannot reload after a create, a migrate or the removal of a broken VM from the
health check, the change is kept and you are told to run
.Ic doas vmctl reload .
Update and delete show the reload error as it is.
.Pp
If
.Ic vmd \-n
already fails before
.Nm
changes anything,
.Nm
refuses create, update, delete and migrate and shows the error.
Removing a broken VM from the health check and the network setup still work.
Hand-written configuration is never repaired.
.Sh NETWORKING
A VM has one network setting, Internet on or off.
On means the block has a
.Ic local interface :
.Xr vmd 8
gives the guest an address in 100.64.0.0/10 and runs DHCP.
Off means no interface at all.
Nothing else (switches, bridges, port forwarding) is managed.
.Pp
So the guests can reach the internet,
.Nm
sets up the host once, when the first VM with Internet is created or migrated.
It asks first and shows what it will do:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Writes
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
match out on egress inet from 100.64.0.0/10 to any nat-to (egress)
pass in quick inet proto { udp tcp } from 100.64.0.0/10 to any port domain rdr-to 127.0.0.1 port domain
block return in quick on ! lo0 proto { tcp udp } from ! 100.64.0.0/10 to port { sunrpc nfsd }
.Ed
.It
Appends to
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
this marked block, then loads it with
.Ic pfctl \-f :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# added by vmmanager
anchor "vmmanager"
load anchor "vmmanager" from "/etc/vmmanager/pf.conf"
.Ed
.It
Turns on IP forwarding with
.Ic sysctl net.inet.ip.forwarding=1
and adds that line to
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf .
.It
Enables and starts
.Xr unwind 8
if neither it nor unbound is running.
Guests ask the host for DNS and pf redirects the queries to the resolver on
127.0.0.1.
.El
.Pp
A missing
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
is never created; the setup is refused.
If loading the rules fails, both files are restored.
.Pp
The check at start-up needs no root and runs only if you own a managed VM
with Internet.
It only tests that
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf
matches the current rules (an older file is offered as
.Dq Update the vmmanager pf rules ) ,
that IP forwarding is on, and that unwind or unbound is running.
If the pf rules were flushed by hand, NAT stays off until
.Ic doas pfctl \-f /etc/pf.conf
or a reboot, and
.Nm
does not detect it.
.Pp
The anchor is loaded at the end of the main ruleset, so its rules win over
earlier rules that are not
.Ic quick ;
earlier
.Ic quick
rules still take precedence.
Hand-written NAT or
.Ic rdr-to
lines are left alone.
If you had your own NAT rules for 100.64.0.0/10, you can delete them from
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
and run
.Ic doas pfctl \-f /etc/pf.conf ;
the anchor now does the job.
.Pp
To undo everything:
delete the three marked lines from
.Pa /etc/pf.conf ,
run
.Ic doas rm /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf ,
remove
.Li net.inet.ip.forwarding=1
from
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf ,
reload with
.Ic doas pfctl \-f /etc/pf.conf ,
and if you do not want the resolver,
.Ic doas rcctl disable unwind
and
.Ic doas rcctl stop unwind .
Then
.Ic doas sysctl net.inet.ip.forwarding=0 .
.Sh SHARED FOLDERS
Key
.Ic f
shares a folder of the host with one VM over NFS.
The VM needs Internet on, because the share uses the same
.Ic local interface
and the pf rules as
.Sx NETWORKING .
Only that VM can mount the folder, and files written in the guest belong to the
user who owns the VM on the host.
.Pp
The first time, and again whenever something below is missing,
.Nm
asks first and lists what it will do, all with
.Xr doas 1 .
If the pf rules of
.Sx NETWORKING
are missing or outdated, the question also names each change of the network setup
(the lines of
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf
and
.Pa /etc/pf.conf ,
IP forwarding in
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf ,
the resolver) before anything runs.
The setup steps are:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Installs
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/sync-exports
and adds a line that runs it to
.Pa /etc/rc.local ,
under a
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment.
.It
Sets the
.Xr nfsd 8
flags to
.Li \-t \-n 4
(TCP only, 4 server processes) and enables
.Xr portmap 8 ,
.Xr mountd 8
and
.Xr nfsd 8 .
They are not started yet:
.Xr mountd 8
and
.Xr nfsd 8
refuse to start while
.Pa /etc/exports
has no line that is not a comment.
.Nm
starts whichever of the three is not running after the first share has
written its line.
.It
Updates
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf
with a line that blocks the NFS ports (sunrpc and nfsd) from everything
outside 100.64.0.0/10, and reloads it.
.El
.Pp
Sharing a folder writes
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.share
and runs the sync script, which holds the lock
.Pa /var/run/exports.lock ,
reads the VM ids from
.Ic vmctl status ,
and rewrites its lines in
.Pa /etc/exports
before it signals
.Xr mountd 8 .
Each shared VM gets two lines, a marker and the export:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# vmmanager: the next line is managed by vmmanager (<name>)
/home/user/folder \-mapall=<user> 100.64.<id>.3
.Ed
.Pp
The address 100.64.<id>.3 is the one
.Xr vmd 8
gives that VM, so no other VM can mount the folder.
Lines without the marker are never touched.
Stopping the share, deleting the VM or turning its Internet off removes the
lines again.
The folder must exist and its path must be absolute, without spaces,
.Ql # ,
.Ql \e ,
.Ql $ ,
.Ql % ,
backticks or quotes.
.Nm
refuses to share
.Pa /
(the whole filesystem) and says to choose a folder inside it.
.Pp
The VM's ids change when
.Xr vmd 8
restarts.
.Nm
therefore reruns the sync script at boot, from
.Pa /etc/rc.local .
.Pp
Boot window:
.Xr mountd 8
reads the old
.Pa /etc/exports
before
.Xr vmd 8
assigns the new ids, and the sync script corrects it some seconds later.
For that time a line can point at the wrong VM.
VMs made by
.Nm
never start at boot (they are
.Ic disable ) ,
so only a hand-written VM that is
.Ic enable Ns d
could be affected, and the VM that
.Xr dockerctl 8
starts from
.Va pkg_scripts ,
which runs before
.Pa /etc/rc.local .
A stale line can, until the sync, point at whatever VM now has that id.
.Pp
The guest side is not set up by
.Nm
because it needs root in the guest.
Run these there, as root; the host is the guest's default gateway.
Debian and Ubuntu need
.Ic apt install nfs-common .
To mount the folder now, in a Linux guest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkdir -p /mnt/share
mount -t nfs -o vers=3,proto=tcp,nolock "$(/sbin/ip route | awk '/default/{print $3}'):FOLDER" /mnt/share
.Ed
.Pp
and in an OpenBSD guest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkdir -p /mnt/share
mount_nfs -T "$(/sbin/route -n get default | awk '/gateway:/{print $2}'):FOLDER" /mnt/share
.Ed
.Pp
Replace FOLDER with the shared path.
To mount it at every guest boot, add one line with
.Ic crontab \-e
as root.
Linux:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
@reboot mkdir -p /mnt/share; for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do mount -t nfs -o vers=3,proto=tcp,nolock "$(/sbin/ip route | awk '/default/{print $3}'):FOLDER" /mnt/share && break; sleep 10; done
.Ed
.Pp
OpenBSD:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
@reboot mkdir -p /mnt/share; for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do /sbin/mount_nfs -T "$(/sbin/route -n get default | awk '/gateway:/{print $2}'):FOLDER" /mnt/share && break; sleep 10; done
.Ed
.Pp
The loop retries because the network may not be up yet at boot.
.Pp
.Xr mountd 8
has no fixed port on OpenBSD 8.0, so pf cannot block it by port.
Someone on the local network who scans for it can read the export list, but
cannot mount a folder, as only the VM's address is allowed.
.Pp
The health check says
.Dq Shared folders are out of date
if
.Pa /etc/exports
does not match the
.Pa *.share
files, for example after someone edited it by hand.
The fix
.Dq Update shares
runs the sync script.
.Sh MIGRATING
A VM that is in
.Xr vm.conf 5
but was not created by
.Nm
is listed greyed out and has one action, migrate
.Pq Ic m .
It is never done automatically.
It is offered only when the block's
.Ic owner
is you, or there is no
.Ic owner
line; in that case
.Ic owner <user>
is added.
.Pp
The VM must be stopped.
.Nm
then:
.Bl -enum
.It
Moves the disks into
.Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>/
(a rename, which keeps owner and mode).
.It
Writes the block, copied as it was but with the new disk paths, to
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf
and adds it to the aggregate file.
.It
Adds the include line to
.Pa /etc/vm.conf
if missing, and comments out the old block there with the marker
.Li # moved to vmmanager YYYY-MM-DD: .
Nothing is deleted.
.It
Checks with
.Ic vmd \-n
and reloads.
On failure the disks are moved back and every file is restored.
.El
.Pp
Migration is refused, with the reason shown, when:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the VM is running or paused;
.It
the block uses macros, is an instance of a template, or shares a line with
other text;
.It
the block is not in
.Pa /etc/vm.conf
(for example it is in another included file);
.It
the owner is another user, or the name is already managed;
.It
a disk is missing, is a qcow2 with a backing file, is on another filesystem
than your home, is in a folder you cannot write to, or two disks have the same
file name;
.It
a disk or the ISO is not a regular file owned by you or root (see
.Sx SECURITY ) ,
or the ISO is missing;
.It
its
.Xr vm.conf 5
block has a layout
.Nm
cannot edit;
.It
the folder
.Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>
already exists.
.El
.Pp
To migrate by hand:
.Bl -enum
.It
Stop the VM.
.It
Run
.Ic mkdir \-p ~/vmmanager/<name>
and move the disks there.
.It
As root, create
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf
with the vm block, the new disk paths and
.Ic owner <user> .
.It
Add
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf"
to
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf ,
and
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf"
to
.Pa /etc/vm.conf
if missing.
.It
Comment out the old block in
.Pa /etc/vm.conf .
.It
Run
.Ic doas vmd \-n ,
then
.Ic doas vmctl reload .
.El
.Sh SECURITY
.Xr vmd 8
opens disk and ISO files as root.
A symbolic link swapped in for a disk, pointing at a device such as
.Pa /dev/sd0c ,
would give the guest the host's disk.
Before it starts a VM,
.Nm
therefore checks every disk and ISO path in the VM's block: each, including the ISO, must be a
regular file, not a symbolic link or device, owned by the VM's owner or root.
Otherwise the VM is not started and the files are listed.
.Pp
Only the owner of a VM may act on it.
Other users' VMs are shown read-only.
Since every owner already has
.Xr doas 1 ,
this is defence in depth, not a barrier against an administrator.
.Pp
A config file writable by an ordinary user would be read by
.Xr vmd 8
as root, so everything under
.Pa /etc/vmmanager
is written through
.Xr doas 1
and owned by root.
.Sh HEALTH CHECK
At start-up
.Nm
checks, without asking for root:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
that the
.Xr vmd 8
socket is usable;
.It
that
.Xr vmd 8
is running, and offers
.Ic rcctl enable
and
.Ic rcctl start
if not;
.It
that
.Pa /etc/firmware/vmm-bios
exists, and offers
.Xr fw_update 8
if not;
.It
that the network setup is complete, see
.Sx NETWORKING ;
.It
that
.Ic vmd \-n
passes.
If a VM folder was deleted by hand, the check names the broken VM and, for
its owner, offers to remove its configuration;
.It
that your shared folders match
.Pa /etc/exports ,
only when
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/sync-exports
exists.
If not, it says "Shared folders are out of date" and offers
.Ic Update shares ,
which runs the sync script.
.El
.Sh UNINSTALL
Do the steps in this order, so that
.Xr pf.conf 5
and
.Xr vm.conf 5
never point at files that are gone.
.Bl -enum
.It
If you shared folders, stop sharing every VM first: press
.Ic f
on each shared VM with Internet on and choose to stop sharing.
By hand instead, delete each
.Li # vmmanager:
marker line and the line after it from
.Pa /etc/exports ,
then run
.Ic doas pkill \-HUP \-x mountd .
.It
Remove the program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pipx uninstall openbsd-vmmanager
.Ed
.It
Remove the man pages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas rm /usr/local/man/man8/vmmanager.8 /usr/local/man/man5/vmmanager.toml.5 && doas makewhatis /usr/local/man
.Ed
.It
Remove the three marked lines (the
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment, the
.Ic anchor
line and the
.Ic load anchor
line) from
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
and the
.Li net.inet.ip.forwarding=1
line from
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf
if you do not want it, then run
.Ic doas pfctl \-f /etc/pf.conf .
See
.Sx NETWORKING
for the resolver.
.It
Remove the line
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf"
and its
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment from
.Pa /etc/vm.conf ,
run
.Ic doas vmd \-n
and
.Ic doas vmctl reload .
.It
If you shared folders, delete the line that runs
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/sync-exports
(and its
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment) from
.Pa /etc/rc.local ,
then remove the script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas rm /etc/vmmanager/sync-exports
.Ed
.Pp
Then run
.Ic doas rcctl disable portmap mountd nfsd
and
.Ic doas rcctl stop portmap mountd nfsd ,
unless
.Xr dockerctl 8
or you use NFS for something else.
.It
Only now delete the configuration:
.Ic doas rm \-r /etc/vmmanager .
.It
Your VMs' disks stay in
.Pa ~/vmmanager ,
which you may keep or delete.
.El
.Pp
To give a migrated VM back to a hand-written
.Pa /etc/vm.conf ,
note that its commented-out block, marked
.Li # moved to vmmanager ,
still holds the old disk paths.
Move the disks back first (or edit the paths in the block), remove the VM's
file under
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>
and its include line from
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf ,
uncomment the block in
.Pa /etc/vm.conf ,
then run
.Ic doas vmd \-n
and
.Ic doas vmctl reload .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr doas.conf 5 ,
.Xr pf.conf 5 ,
.Xr vm.conf 5 ,
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5 ,
.Xr unwind 8 ,
.Xr vmctl 8 ,
.Xr vmd 8
